    "version": "0.1.1-m10-contract",
    "suite": "D-set (dual-code routing)",
    "note": "v0.1 seed. Paper-grade D-set: ≥60 real cases stratified by type, including ≥20 ICNP-preemption cases (must be 100% correct). Full build is an M1 deliverable. For the ICNP-occupied cases the expected values encode 'The SeqCode recognises ICNP priority' is registration/curatorial practice, not an article of either code: the two codes are independent and each computes priority within itself, so route() must surface the ICNP conflict-guidance entry FIRST while keeping the SeqCode path visible and labelled, rather than hard-returning a single ICNP-only path. Changed expectations: the three is_icnp_preemption cases moved from [\"ICNP\"] to [\"ICNP\", \"SeqCode\"] (both role conflict-guidance), and the two unknown-source cases from [] to the router's needs-source gate entry ([\"unknown\"], plus [\"unknown\", \"ICNP\", \"SeqCode\"] when icnp_occupied is true). The lists are positional (path i is expected_codes[i] + expected_roles[i]) and ICNP-guidance-first, matching the order router.py guarantees; evaluator.evaluate_d_set() still compares them as sets, so tests/test_d_set_contract.py is what pins the order and the pairing. IMPORTANT: the pre-emption advice itself remains PENDING EXPERT SIGN-OFF on its wording (see routing/router.py::PREEMPTION_PROVENANCE ) — these values record the two-path routing contract, not a settled legal ruling. The per-case note 'ICNP pre-emption: must NOT route to SeqCode' on the first preemption case is wording kept verbatim for annotation provenance; it describes the warning that must always be present, not a bar that hides the SeqCode path.",
    "target_sizes": {
